+# Headless Dialog primitive
+
+This package owns the behavior shared by the three product dialogs: open state,
+modal semantics, portals, focus containment/restoration, Escape handling and
+nested-layer ordering. Product copy, actions, layout and visual tokens remain in
+the consuming product.
+
+## Public API
+
+```tsx
+import { Dialog } from 'headless-dialog-primitive-fixture'
+
+<Dialog.Root defaultOpen={false} onOpenChange={(open) => {}}>
+  <Dialog.Trigger className="productButton">Edit profile</Dialog.Trigger>
+  <Dialog.Portal>
+    <Dialog.Overlay className="productOverlay" />
+    <Dialog.Content className="productDialog">
+      <Dialog.Title>Edit profile</Dialog.Title>
+      <Dialog.Description>Update your public information.</Dialog.Description>
+      {/* Product fields and actions belong here. */}
+      <Dialog.Close className="productButton">Cancel</Dialog.Close>
+    </Dialog.Content>
+  </Dialog.Portal>
+</Dialog.Root>
+```
+
+`Root` supports uncontrolled state through `defaultOpen` and controlled state
+through `open` plus `onOpenChange`. `Content.initialFocusRef` selects a specific
+initial target; otherwise the first focusable descendant is used. Pass
+`aria-describedby={null}` when the dialog intentionally has no description.
+`Portal.container` can target a product-owned layer host. All DOM parts accept
+native attributes, `className`, `style`, event handlers and forwarded refs, and
+expose `data-state` without shipping visual CSS.
+
+Named exports (`DialogRoot`, `DialogContent`, and the other parts and prop types)
+are available from the same package entry for consumers that prefer direct
+imports. Internal focus/layer utilities are deliberately not exported.
+
+## Runtime boundaries and invariants
+
+- The root owns only open state and stable semantic IDs. Controlled state is
+  never mirrored into an independent state source.
+- The portal is absent on the server and on the client's first hydration render;
+  it mounts after hydration, so browser globals are not read during SSR.
+- One internal stack per `Document` owns Escape, Tab containment, background
+  `inert`/`aria-hidden`, scroll locking and cleanup. Only the top layer acts.
+- Closing a nested layer restores its own trigger. Closing the last layer
+  restores the outer trigger and the exact pre-existing body/ARIA state.
+- `Title` and `Description` provide the IDs used by `Content`. Consumer-provided
+  copy, buttons and styles never enter the primitive.
+
+The package ships ESM plus declarations from `dist/package`; React and React DOM
+are peer dependencies so all consumers share the host renderer.
+
+## Verification matrix
+
+| Contract | SSR test | Chromium behavior test | Type/build/package check |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Closed and initially-open server render avoids browser globals | Yes | Hydrated example | Yes |
+| Controlled and uncontrolled open/close | Markup | Parent controlled; child uncontrolled | Public prop types |
+| Accessible name, description and modal state | IDs in markup | Role/name/description assertions | Declarations |
+| Initial focus, forward/reverse Tab loop and restoration | — | Yes | — |
+| Escape affects only the top nested dialog | — | Yes | — |
+| Background isolation and scroll cleanup | — | Yes | — |
+| Overlay dismissal does not treat content clicks as outside | — | Yes | — |
+| Consumer styling and refs/native props | — | Fixture classes | Exported DOM prop types |
+| Publish artifact and peer dependency boundary | — | — | Build plus dry-run pack |
+
+Before release, run `npm run check`. This fixture's supported browser proof is
+Chromium; add Firefox/WebKit jobs before claiming those browsers in a product
+support matrix.
+
+## Incremental migration
+
+1. Add this package beside each product's existing dialog and migrate one low-risk
+   dialog at a time. Keep that product's copy, actions and CSS unchanged.
+2. Replace its local state wrapper with `Root`, its opener with `Trigger`, and
+   move its existing overlay/content DOM into `Portal`, `Overlay` and `Content`.
+   Replace title/description/close elements with the corresponding parts.
+3. If the product already controls visibility, pass its current `open` value and
+   update callback. Otherwise use `defaultOpen`; do not keep both old and new
+   state owners.
+4. Remove that dialog's document key listeners, focus trap, body scroll lock and
+   portal code only after its keyboard, nested and close-path tests pass against
+   the primitive. A product can roll back independently until this step.
+5. Repeat for the other products. Retire the old shared/local implementations
+   only after all call sites and their product-specific visual checks migrate.
+
+A later requirement such as an alert dialog can be added as a separate semantic
+part or narrow `role` policy without changing the layer stack; animation can wrap
+the portal parts while preserving mount/focus ownership.
